export default "Hostinger API notes for agents. Find operations with search; the inputSchema it returns is authoritative for request shapes. Run one with execute, or chain several with multi-execute.\n\nDatabases (web hosting, hosting_* operations)\n- MySQL runs on the same server as the website. From the deployed app connect to host 127.0.0.1, port 3306. PHP may also use localhost (Unix socket). Node.js should use 127.0.0.1: localhost can resolve to IPv6 (::1) and not every database user has that grant.\n- The `host` from hosting_databases_list (srvNNNN.hstgr.io) is only for connections from outside Hostinger, after hosting_databases_create-remote-connection. Never use it from the app.\n- Agency Plan (agency-hosting_* operations) database users are created for localhost; the PHP app connects there.\n\nAsynchronous operations\n- A success response often means \"queued\", not \"done\". Poll the matching read operation with backoff (seconds, then minutes). Never re-send the write.\n- hosting_websites_create -> poll hosting_websites_list-setups with the domain until status is completed (a few minutes); file, deploy and database calls return 404/409 before that.\n- A Node.js deploy or hosting_nodejs_start-build -> poll hosting_get-deployment with the domain until the build state is completed or failed; a failed build comes back with its logs and failure analysis.\n- wordpress_installations_install and WordPress plugin/theme/core jobs -> poll wordpress_installations_list.\n- agency-hosting_website-setups_create -> poll agency-hosting_website-setups_status with setup_uuid; other Agency Plan jobs -> agency-hosting_websites_list-processes.\n- Website deletion, database repair and domain contact changes finish in the background.\n\nCredentials\n- Never write database passwords or API tokens into source code, client-side JavaScript or committed .env files.\n- Node.js apps read them from environment variables: set the full set with hosting_nodejs_replace-environment-variables (full replace; values are masked on read). Build-time variables (Next.js) need a new build afterwards.\n- PHP apps read them from a config file on the server, outside the repository.\n- Generate strong passwords and do not echo them back once stored.\n\nDeploying from a shell\n- The Hostinger CLI deploys a website in one command and links the directory: `hostinger up <dir> --domain <domain> --format json` (`--yes` replaces content, `--create` makes a site, `--dry-run` shows the plan). It never prompts without a terminal.\n\nFor end-to-end site builds install the headless skill: npx skills add hostinger/api-mcp-server";
